WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.070
Every morning our school bus

2
00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.710
picks up children in nine

3
00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.370
villages before lessons start.

4
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.890
The driver always goes to the

5
00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.190
nearest village next.

6
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.680
It’s quick to plan, but is

7
00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:12.860
it the shortest round?

8
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.460
Today: quick rules, and

9
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:17.760
the traps they hide.

10
00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:21.030
A quick rule makes a plan

11
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.910
without trying every plan.

12
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.980
Nearest village next.

13
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.340
Most points per kilo first.

14
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:28.880
Favourite first.

15
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.010
Quick rules are fast, easy to

16
00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:33.610
explain, and often good.

17
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.280
The bus rule is called nearest neighbour:

18
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.860
from the school, drive to the closest

19
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.220
village, then the closest one not yet

20
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.520
visited, and so on, then back.

21
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:46.560
On our map it drives

22
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.540
thirty-four point six kilometres.

23
00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.470
But it never looks ahead, so the last

24
00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.470
legs back to school are long.

25
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:56.780
Now improve it.

26
00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:00.120
Find two roads that cross, take them

27
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.140
out, and join the stretch between

28
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.500
them the other way round.

29
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.320
Keep the swap if the round gets shorter.

30
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:08.650
Three swaps bring it down to

31
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.170
twenty-eight point six kilometres.

32
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.390
Twenty-eight point six is the

33
00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:14.350
best round there is.

34
00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.890
So the quick rule drove about a fifth too

35
00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.690
far: six kilometres every morning.

36
00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.850
That’s about thirty kilometres

37
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.790
of fuel a week, all year.

38
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.680
Why not just try every round?

39
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.690
With nine villages there are one

40
00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.610
hundred and eighty-one thousand,

41
00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.150
four hundred and forty, and a

42
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.690
computer checks those in a blink.

43
00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.060
With twenty villages there are more than a

44
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.220
billion billion: at a billion a second,

45
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.980
that’s about thirty-eight years.

46
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.770
So real planners make a quick

47
00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:49.310
route, then improve it.

48
00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:50.940
Quick check!

49
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.120
A quick rule gives me a

50
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.300
plan in one second.

51
00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.510
What do I know about that plan?

52
00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.700
That it’s usually good, but

53
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:00.920
maybe not the best.

54
00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.010
So check it: compare it with a better

55
00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.430
method, or try small improvements.

56
00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.760
In the truck lab, most points per

57
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.880
kilo first gives sixty points, and

58
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.880
the best load gives sixty-five.

59
00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.300
Quick rules can also be unfair.

60
00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.290
Nearest first picks up the closest

61
00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.950
children first, so they ride the bus the

62
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:24.210
longest.

63
00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.900
On a long round, a small

64
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.800
child may sit for an hour.

65
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.140
Check who pays for a rule,

66
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:32.460
not only the total.

67
00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.810
In the app, press Run and watch the bus

68
00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.970
take the nearest village each time.

69
00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:42.450
Then watch the swaps untangle the route.

70
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.090
Press New map and try again: sometimes

71
00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:48.170
the swaps stop before the best.

72
00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:49.950
Your turn.

73
00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.040
The bus round went from thirty-four

74
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.720
point six kilometres to twenty-eight

75
00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.600
point six after three swaps.

76
00:02:58.250 --> 00:02:59.430
What did the swaps do?

77
00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.260
They untangled the crossing roads, and

78
00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.140
every village is still visited.

79
00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:08.630
Remember: quick rules are fast and

80
00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:11.590
often good, but not always the best.

81
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.420
Improve a plan with small swaps.

82
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:17.220
And check who a rule is hard on.

83
00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.030
Try it in class tomorrow:

84
00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:21.850
make a human bus route.

85
00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.600
Nine pupils stand around the room as

86
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.140
villages, and a bus pupil walks

87
00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.140
nearest first, counting steps.

88
00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.540
Then find paths that cross,

89
00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.700
swap them, and count again.
